  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 255 AM MST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nWe find ourselves in a ridging pattern once again to start the work \nweek with a trough exiting the eastern US. The first in a sequence \nof two longwave ridges builds in throughout the Great Plains Monday, \naccompanied by northwest flow aloft, as we initially sit on the \nleeward side. This system is then expected to weaken quite a bit as \nthe axis passes north of the County Warning Area (CWA), resulting in \nzonal westerly flow. Ensemble guidance continues to project stronger \nridging to build in across the Rockies that likely ushers in \nnorthwest flow as we position east of the axis. Models are beginning \nto show us under northerly upper-level winds Thursday as the ridge \ncontinues its trek across the CONUS. High pressure is forecast to \ndominate aloft as well as at the surface through the middle portions \nof next week. A stationary front is anticipated to linger across \nwestern Kansas and eastern Colorado Tuesday through Thursday. \nDespite the lifting mechanism, precipitation (PoPs) in the form\nof rain are highly unlikely through Thursday\n\nNew developments of this second ridge signal that quiet, pleasant \nweather is possible heading into late next week. Subsidence plus low \nmoisture content in the atmosphere should equate to warmer and drier \nconditions as we progress through the week. As mentioned above, no \nrain is currently forecasted through Thursday. Conditions should be \nquite nice for this time of the year as a warmer trend is projected \nto continue across the CWA, paired with plentiful sunshine \nthroughout most of the four-day period. Highs in the mid/upper \n60s are forecast Monday through Thursday for the entire CWA but\na few spots reaching the low 70s are possible as well. Low \ntemps should be cool and crisp every morning of this long-term \nperiod as guidance output suggests mid 30s to mid 40s. There is \nthe possibility of some locations to reach freezing Monday and \nTuesday mornings but confidence is low at this time.\n\nAs drier air is likely to take over our atmosphere, Relative \nHumidity (RH) values will be monitored, with respect to fire weather \nweather concerns, through next week. Guidance currently has a \ngeneral range of 25-45% RH forecasted, but areas west of US-385 in \neastern Colorado could see values in the low 20s. Surface winds are \nnot likely to be very strong next week either, as a range of 5-20 \nmph sustained winds is probable. Wednesday should be the windiest \nday across the CWA with gusts up to 30 mph possible. Furthermore,\nGFDI values indicate that dry fuels are not an issue due to \nrecent rains from earlier this week. Therefore, fire weather \nshould not be much of a concern through next week.",
